ten days after fleeing their home with  their six children  Robina and allie her son have returned  to see what's left of it Robina says  they fled when they saw groups of men  setting houses on fire and the most  stuff a bad neighborhood Quintilian what  it was nothing of ours is saved we just  took our children and ran for our lives  the family is one of at least 1,700 that  have been displaced in Northeast Delhi I  don't know how my children will continue  their studies everything we have worked  for is destroyed I just want all of this  to be over and to start a new life  Robina and her family have been staying  with relatives others are taking shelter  at this nearby relief camp that's been  set up by student and civil society  groups around five hundred people are  staying here everyone has a story  including these cousins Alfia and  upsetter when we were running away they  were throwing stones and that's when I  got hurt they burnt many shops we were  watching from the roof of the house they  even burnt our mosque the olia mustered  in the neighborhood of Shiva hide is one  of at least four mosques that were  destroyed the violence began on February  23rd when fighting started between  Hindus and Muslims protesting against  the citizenship law that critics say  discriminates against Muslims what that  led to violence from both communities a  fact-finding team says Muslims suffered  disproportionately all of them how left  that place but it is Muslim whether it  is Muslim community or the Hindu  community but when you look at the house  as the building the Ender houses of the  Muslim community is damaged no damage to  the Hindu houses Delhi police have  arrested 1600 people but the families of  those killed or injured say they want  politicians to be held to account  a few hours before the violence started  on February 23rd Kapil Mishra a member  of the ruling bat at the Ajanta party  said his supporters would take matters  into their own hands unless those  p